/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/P 36 0 R
/T (The files contain country-level indicators covering population, economic activity, health, education, internet access, environment and homicide. The original workbook has 204 rows and 38 columns. It includes 12 exact duplicate rows, leaving 192 unique country records after deduplication. The cleaned dataset used in the notebook contains 192 countries and 20 selected variables, with no missing values reported in those selected columns.)
/E (The files contain country-level indicators covering population, economic activity, health, education, internet access, environment and homicide. The original workbook has 204 rows and 38 columns. It includes 12 exact duplicate rows, leaving 192 unique country records after deduplication. The cleaned dataset used in the notebook contains 192 countries and 20 selected variables, with no missing values reported in those selected columns.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [24]
>>
endobj
66 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/P 36 0 R
/T (The main substantive finding is that homicide rates differ sharply across countries and regions. In the cleaned analysis, the median homicide rate is 2.5 while the mean is 6.5, showing that a relatively small number of countries with high rates pull the average upward. Central America, the Caribbean and Southern Africa have the highest regional median rates in the notebook's analysis. Several health, education and development indicators move together, but no single indicator explains homicide rates on its own.)
/E (The main substantive finding is that homicide rates differ sharply across countries and regions. In the cleaned analysis, the median homicide rate is 2.5 while the mean is 6.5, showing that a relatively small number of countries with high rates pull the average upward. Central America, the Caribbean and Southern Africa have the highest regional median rates in the notebook's analysis. Several health, education and development indicators move together, but no single indicator explains homicide rates on its own.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [25]
>>
endobj
