>>
endobj
176 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 39 0 R
/E (The cleaned analysis finds that homicide rate has mostly weak-to-moderate relationships with individual indicators. Spearman correlations are negative for male life expectancy \(-0.43\), female life expectancy \(-0.39\), internet use \(-0.37\) and GDP per capita \(-0.33\). They are positive for infant mortality \(+0.41\), fertility \(+0.34\) and unemployment \(+0.19\).)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
177 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 39 0 R
/E (These figures describe rank-based associations, not cause and effect. The difference between Pearson and Spearman results for some variables suggests that relationships may not be straight-line patterns and may be affected by extreme values. It would be misleading to conclude that changing any one of these indicators would directly change homicide rates.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
