190 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 39 0 R
/E (Taken together, the data presents a broad picture of differences in living conditions and social outcomes between countries. Population size, economic resources, health outcomes, education and access to the internet vary considerably. Several of these indicators are closely related to one another, which is expected when they reflect wider differences in development.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
191 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 39 0 R
/E (Homicide is the most unevenly distributed outcome examined in the notebook. The high regional medians in Central America, the Caribbean and Southern Africa stand out, while most countries fall at or below a rate of 10. Individual economic or social measures show some association with homicide, but the relationships are not strong enough to explain the pattern by themselves. Regional context and other unmeasured factors are likely to matter.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [17]
>>
endobj
192 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 39 0 R
/E (For practical use, the cleaned dataset is a reasonable starting point for descriptive analysis and for developing hypotheses about possible crime hotspots. It is not yet sufficient on its own to establish causes or to make reliable predictions. Before a mapping or prediction project, the ISO codes, imputation history, indicator definitions, units and reference years should be verified.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [18]
>>
endobj
