579 0 obj
<<
/Title (The difference between regions is more pronounced than a single global average suggests. The notebook reports that all other regions have median rates of 3.4 or lower, apart from the higher-rate regions listed above. These comparisons are descriptive; differences in reporting practices, data coverage, population characteristics and other factors may also influence the observed values.)
/Dest [12 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 571 0 R
/Prev 586 0 R
>>
endobj
580 0 obj
<<
/Title (GDP per capita is strongly right-skewed in the cleaned dataset: the notebook reports a mean of about US$15,500 and a median of about US$5,900. A small number of high-income countries pull the mean upward, so the median is a better summary of the middle country in this dataset.)
/Dest [12 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 572 0 R
/Next 581 0 R
>>
endobj
581 0 obj
<<
/Title (Population, population density and CO2 emissions also have long right tails. Unemployment and infant mortality are right-skewed as well, while life expectancy and school enrollment tend to cluster toward the higher end of their observed ranges. These patterns point to substantial differences in country size and development conditions; they also explain why comparisons based only on means can be misleading.)
/Dest [12 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 572 0 R
/Prev 580 0 R
>>
endobj
